<script setup>
import {onMounted,ref} from 'vue';
import {directions,projects} from './content';
import './DesignFrame.vue';
const tab=ref('designs');
const videoReady=ref({});
onMounted(async()=>{
  document.title='Four portfolio designs | Matthew Lack';
  await Promise.all(directions.map(async d=>{try{const r=await fetch(`/design-assets/demos/${d.id}.mp4`,{method:'HEAD'});videoReady.value[d.id]=r.ok&&r.headers.get('content-type')?.includes('video');}catch{videoReady.value[d.id]=false;}}));
});
</script>

<template>
  <div class="design-review"><header class="dr-header"><span>Matthew Lack / Portfolio redesign</span><RouterLink to="/">View current site ↗</RouterLink></header><main>
    <section class="dr-intro"><span class="dr-kicker">Design review · October 2026</span><h1>Four ways to show<br>what you've made.</h1><p>Same projects, different ways in. Browse the live designs, watch the walkthroughs, and decide which one feels like you.</p><div class="dr-facts"><span>{{ projects.length }} real projects</span><span>Desktop & mobile layouts</span><span>Local, working previews</span></div></section>
    <div class="dr-tabs" aria-label="Review view"><button :class="{active:tab==='designs'}" :aria-pressed="tab==='designs'" @click="tab='designs'">The four designs</button><button :class="{active:tab==='research'}" :aria-pressed="tab==='research'" @click="tab='research'">Research & decisions</button></div>
    <section v-if="tab==='designs'" class="dr-directions"><article v-for="d in directions" :key="d.id" class="dr-direction"><RouterLink :to="`/designs/${d.id}`" class="dr-preview" :class="d.id"><img :src="`/design-assets/previews/${d.id}.jpg`" :alt="`${d.name} desktop preview`" loading="lazy" @error="event=>event.target.style.display='none'" /><div class="dr-preview-fallback"><span>{{ d.number }}</span><strong>{{ d.name }}</strong></div><span class="dr-preview-open">Open live design ↗</span></RouterLink><div class="dr-direction-content"><div class="dr-direction-title"><span>{{ d.number }}</span><h2>{{ d.name }}</h2></div><p class="dr-short">{{ d.short }}</p><p>{{ d.description }}</p><ul><li v-for="change in d.changes" :key="change">{{ change }}</li></ul><p class="dr-tradeoff">{{ d.tradeoff }}</p><RouterLink :to="`/designs/${d.id}`" class="dr-open">Try this design <span>↗</span></RouterLink><details v-if="videoReady[d.id]" class="dr-video"><summary>Watch the demo <span>Desktop, interactions & mobile</span></summary><video controls preload="none" :poster="`/design-assets/previews/${d.id}.jpg`"><source :src="`/design-assets/demos/${d.id}.mp4`" type="video/mp4" /><track kind="captions" :src="`/design-assets/demos/${d.id}.vtt`" srclang="en" label="English" />Your browser does not support video. <a :href="`/design-assets/demos/${d.id}.mp4`">Download the demo</a></video><a :href="`/design-assets/demos/${d.id}.mp4`" download>Download video ↓</a></details></div></article></section>
    <section v-else class="dr-research"><h2>What I took from the research</h2><p>Repeated styling is a weak substitute for deciding what a portfolio should show. These concepts start with the projects and give each layout a different job: reading, inspecting, browsing, or scanning.</p><div class="dr-research-grid"><article><h3>Design</h3><p>Commentary on AI websites repeatedly calls out interchangeable card grids, glowing gradients, default typography, and decoration without a purpose. Changing a color alone does little to change that structure.</p><p>Here, project screenshots and board photographs supply the imagery. Each direction has its own composition, type hierarchy, navigation, and way of opening a project.</p><a href="https://blog.interfacekit.io/why-ai-generated-websites-all-look-the-same" target="_blank" rel="noopener">InterfaceKit: repeated website patterns ↗</a><a href="https://www.joshuasnoddy.com/blog/why-ai-websites-look-the-same/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joshua Snoddy: common design defaults ↗</a></article><article><h3>Writing</h3><p>Research on language-model vocabulary documents the overuse of words such as “delve” and “intricate.” No single word or punctuation mark reliably proves how a sentence was written.</p><p>The useful editing rule is to say what you made, what it does, and what your role was. The summaries use ordinary language, omit broad claims about expertise, and keep unfinished work clearly described.</p><a href="https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.426/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">COLING 2025: vocabulary overrepresentation ↗</a><a href="https://paulgraham.com/simply.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paul Graham: Write Simply ↗</a></article></div><h2>What changed</h2><p>The current site introduces skills first and asks visitors to scroll to find the work. All four proposals lead with projects, give the University of Toronto background a shorter home, and keep resume and GitHub links easy to reach.</p><p>Project titles and summaries were edited locally for clarity. Your full technical descriptions remain available on the project pages. No new employers, outcomes, or performance figures have been invented.</p><h2>What is in this preview</h2><p>The previews use a read-only snapshot from your backend, taken on October 9, 2026. All 17 projects and 45 images are available locally. The existing website, authentication, analytics, and project editor remain available at their original routes.</p><p>These are choices to review before selecting a final design. They have not been deployed, and the preview content does not write to the production backend.</p></section>
    <footer class="dr-footer"><span>Pick a direction, or combine the parts you like.</span><RouterLink to="/">Compare with the current site ↗</RouterLink></footer>
  </main></div>
</template>

